        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-sas-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 6 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Hours-use bands. The sheet prices kWh in bands bounded by hours times the Billing Demand: not greater than 200 hours (in blocks of kWh) and over 200 hours. A urpx:ServiceBracket bound is a fixed number (https://github.com/urpx-org/urpx/issues/87), so each band's kWh is a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod, and the first band carries the kWh blocks; a consumer has to split the month's kWh by the Billing Demand. clj-urpx sums every energy ledger for a marginal rate, and the rider subtotals sum the same prices, so this filing's recorded marginal rate adds each higher band's price to the first block's, where the sheet prices a month's first kWh at the first block's price alone. (2) Fuel and On-Peak seasons by billing month. FCR-27 prices June through September and October through May, which Schedule R's bills show is read by the billing month a bill is rendered in, and SAS-17's On-Peak period falls in \"the billing months June through September\". The JSON-LD gates the fuel PriceDefinitions and the On-Peak period on calendar months, so usage in the weeks a billing period runs ahead of its billing month takes the other season's prices. (3) Voltage level and franchise fee location. FCR-27's three voltage levels and MFF-11's two locations are crossed in one ProfileAlternatives set of six alternatives, because URPX v0.5.1 allows one set per PlanElements and the franchise fee's energy subtotal must include that voltage level's fuel prices. A consumer selects one alternative, for example Secondary Distribution inside city limits. (4) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 and DSM-C-15 amounts. Its multiplier Prices here are based on the energy-and-fuel subtotal and on the non-energy charges' Prices, not on the ECCR and DSM-C multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on those two amounts, about 0.44 percent of the base bill inside city limits, is not carried. (5) Minimum Monthly Bill. The sheet sets it at the $43.72 Basic Service Charge plus ECCR, DSM and the Municipal Franchise Fee, and no Price carries it. Every price here is positive, so a bill on these prices alone is never below it. (6) Term of Contract. Five years. Nothing is priced by it and it is not carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
